@meta
id: 20-15
title: What you just ran
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-named
par: 61
minutes: 5
vscode: retype the find-and-replace into a comment and hope it matches
open: seed.sql

@brief
Three more read-only registers record what you just did. `":` holds the
last command line you ran, without the colon. `"/` holds the last
search pattern. `".` holds the last text you typed in insert mode. They
exist so you can put a thing you did into the file, exactly, instead of
remembering it.

The `customer_ref` column was renamed to `customer_id` in the last
migration and this seed file has not caught up. Rename it everywhere in
one substitute. Then, on a new line directly under the header comment,
record exactly what you ran, in the form `-- applied: %s/.../.../g`,
by putting the register rather than retyping the command.

@keys
| `":p` | put the last command line |
| `"/p` | put the last search pattern |
| `".p` | put the last inserted text |
| `@:` | run the last command line again |

@hints
Run the substitute first. Whatever you type after the colon is what
`":` will hold, so type it the way you want it recorded.
--
`gg` back to the header, `o` to open the line under it, type the
prefix, `Esc`, then `":p`.
--
`:%s/customer_ref/customer_id/g` Enter, `gg`, `o-- applied: ` `Esc`,
`":p`.

@solution
    :%s/customer_ref/customer_id/g<Enter>  gg o-- applied: <Esc> ":p  :wq<Enter>

The recorded line cannot disagree with what actually ran, which is the
point of putting it rather than typing it. The same register is what
`@:` replays, so a long substitute or `:g` command you want to run
again on another range is one keystroke away rather than a retype.

`"/` after that substitute holds `customer_ref` — a substitute sets the
search pattern too, which is why `n` finds the next old name after
`:s`. `".` holds `-- applied: `, the last thing you typed in insert
mode; it is the register behind the `.` command's typing, and `".p`
is occasionally the quickest way to type the same words on a line where
`.` would do the wrong thing.

@start seed.sql
-- seed: orders and refunds for the local environment

INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_ref, status, total) VALUES ('ord_001', 'cus_042', 'PENDING', 1999);
INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_ref, status, total) VALUES ('ord_002', 'cus_017', 'SHIPPED', 4500);
INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_ref, status, total) VALUES ('ord_003', 'cus_042', 'CANCELLED', 2750);
INSERT INTO refunds (id, order_id, customer_ref, amount) VALUES ('ref_001', 'ord_003', 'cus_042', 2750);

@expected seed.sql
-- seed: orders and refunds for the local environment
-- applied: %s/customer_ref/customer_id/g

INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_001', 'cus_042', 'PENDING', 1999);
INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_002', 'cus_017', 'SHIPPED', 4500);
INSERT INTO orders (id, customer_id, status, total) VALUES ('ord_003', 'cus_042', 'CANCELLED', 2750);
INSERT INTO refunds (id, order_id, customer_id, amount) VALUES ('ref_001', 'ord_003', 'cus_042', 2750);
